WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.013
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:16.795
Unit twenty-three: Maritime Trade Routes. Today you will emphasise double effects with 'not only … but also'.

4
00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:19.667
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.728
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.364
Trade.

7
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.640
Trade means the buying and selling of goods.

8
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.714
Trade connected distant civilizations.

9
00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:34.853
Your turn. Say: Trade.

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:38.480
Trade.

11
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:40.438
Maritime.

12
00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.647
Maritime means connected with the sea.

13
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.656
Maritime routes linked India and China.

14
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.193
Your turn. Say: Maritime.

15
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:54.081
Maritime.

16
00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:55.528
Port.

17
00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:59.294
Port means a town where ships load and unload.

18
00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:02.347
Oc Eo was an important ancient port.

19
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.312
Your turn. Say: Port.

20
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:08.878
Port.

21
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:10.676
Spices.

22
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:15.134
Spices means dried plant products used to flavour food.

23
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.761
Spices were worth more than gold.

24
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.525
Your turn. Say: Spices.

25
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:25.449
Spices.

26
00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:27.057
Silk.

27
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.756
Silk means a fine, smooth fabric made by silkworms.

28
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.965
Chinese silk was traded across the ocean.

29
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.947
Your turn. Say: Silk.

30
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.639
Silk.

31
00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:43.373
Merchant.

32
00:01:44.383 --> 00:01:47.138
Merchant means a person who buys and sells goods.

33
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.678
Arab merchants sailed with the monsoon winds.

34
00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:53.599
Your turn. Say: Merchant.

35
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:57.318
Merchant.

36
00:01:58.083 --> 00:01:59.351
Exchange.

37
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.696
Exchange means the act of giving one thing and receiving another.

38
00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:07.212
Exchange of ideas followed the exchange of goods.

39
00:02:07.817 --> 00:02:10.341
Your turn. Say: Exchange.

40
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.422
Exchange.

41
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:16.092
Network.

42
00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:20.197
Network means a system of connected people or places.

43
00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:23.189
A network of ports stretched to Africa.

44
00:02:23.783 --> 00:02:26.076
Your turn. Say: Network.

45
00:02:28.933 --> 00:02:29.830
Network.

46
00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:34.505
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:40.243
Visal, the Maritime Silk Road passed through Southeast Asia. What did it carry?

48
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:53.012
Trade spread not only goods but also ideas. Ships carried silk and spices, and also monks, scholars and new religions.

49
00:02:54.117 --> 00:02:56.325
How did this affect early Cambodia?

50
00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:07.148
Funan's port at Oc Eo was not only a market but also a centre of cultural exchange, where Indian writing and beliefs arrived.

51
00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.808
And who made it possible?

52
00:03:10.917 --> 00:03:19.560
Not only merchants but also sailors who understood the monsoon winds. The network depended on knowledge as much as on wealth.

53
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.409
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.364
Grammar focus: Not only but also.

55
00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:27.874
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:31.779
Trade spread not only goods but also ideas.

57
00:03:32.683 --> 00:03:37.284
Oc Eo was not only a market but also a centre of cultural exchange.

58
00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:41.274
Not only merchants but also sailors made it possible.

59
00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:53.956
Keep both parts parallel: not only + noun … but also + noun. When 'Not only' begins a clause, invert: 'Not only did trade spread goods, but it also spread ideas.'

60
00:03:54.717 --> 00:03:59.180
Be careful! Don't say: Trade spread not only goods but ideas also.

61
00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:04.665
Say: Trade spread not only goods but also ideas.

62
00:04:04.965 --> 00:04:06.849
Use 'not only … but also …'.

63
00:04:07.633 --> 00:04:10.053
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.867 --> 00:04:12.077
Highways of the Sea.

65
00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:18.788
For more than two thousand years, maritime routes linked the Mediterranean, the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea.

66
00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:26.177
These routes carried not only luxury goods, such as silk and spices, but also religions, languages and technologies.

67
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:32.475
Ports in Southeast Asia became wealthy because ships had to wait there for the monsoon winds to change.

68
00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:38.531
Not only did these ports grow rich, but they also became meeting places for diverse communities.

69
00:04:39.533 --> 00:04:45.167
Maritime trade therefore helped to create the cultural diversity that still characterises the region today.

70
00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:50.547
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:51.383 --> 00:04:54.219
Trade brought not only spices blank new religions.

72
00:04:54.519 --> 00:04:58.172
A, and also. B, but also. C, or also.

73
00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:03.803
The answer is B: but also.

74
00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.983
Not only blank wealth, but it also spread ideas.

75
00:05:08.283 --> 00:05:12.598
A, trade brought. B, did trade bring. C, trade did bring.

76
00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:18.156
The answer is B: did trade bring.

77
00:05:19.317 --> 00:05:21.897
A town where ships load and unload is a blank.

78
00:05:22.197 --> 00:05:25.467
A, merchant. B, port. C, network.

79
00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:30.534
The answer is B: port.

80
00:05:31.683 --> 00:05:33.602
Connected with the sea means blank.

81
00:05:33.902 --> 00:05:37.915
A, maritime. B, silk. C, exchange.

82
00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:43.513
The answer is A: maritime.

83
00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.681
According to the reading, why did Southeast Asian ports become wealthy?

84
00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:54.910
A, ships waited there for the monsoon. B, they produced silk. C, they had no merchants.

85
00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:01.871
The answer is A: ships waited there for the monsoon.

86
00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:06.427
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:07.283 --> 00:06:09.805
How did trade spread culture as well as goods?

88
00:06:26.617 --> 00:06:28.210
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:28.510 --> 00:07:04.351
Trade spread culture because merchants did not only carry products; they also brought their languages, beliefs and customs. When traders stayed in a port for months, waiting for the monsoon, they married local people, built temples and exchanged ideas. In early Cambodia, for example, contact with Indian merchants introduced Sanskrit, Hinduism and new styles of art. Some historians stress that local rulers chose which ideas to adopt. I agree, which shows that cultural exchange was active, not passive.

90
00:07:04.651 --> 00:07:06.279
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:07.183 --> 00:07:11.426
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and not only but also.

92
00:07:11.726 --> 00:07:17.293
Today's value is cultural appreciation. Enjoy and respect the music and art of every culture.

93
00:07:17.593 --> 00:07:20.500
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.546
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

